INDEX -- what is on this disk, and where it came from======================================================Every command here runs with this disk mounted as /dd.  os9exec is thekernel: point it at the disk and name a program, and the disk becomes /dd.  *  A star before a name means that program uses one of Microware's     runtime trap modules -- cio, and for some math or csl.  Those five     modules are on this disk, in CMDS, included with Microware's     permission (SOURCES.txt records the grant), so a starred program     runs here like any other; the star tells you what it depends on.     The grid at the foot of this file lists the starred programs, and     its header carries the count.  A few names appear in more than one     directory, so the grid holds slightly more files than names.  Four programs are BASIC09 I-code: bio, blackjack, dedit and  wysetime.  They are I-code modules (type 2, language 2) and need  Microware's runb to interpret them, the way a starred program needs  cio.  Run them by bare name with runb loaded -- `load runb` then  `bio`.  Giving runb a pathname instead raises BASIC09 error 43,  "Unknown procedure".  BASIC09's own codes are 10-80 and are not OS-9 error numbers.  SYS/errmsg carries text for them anyway -- 67 entries, 000:010 to  000:080 -- so `error 43' reads as "Unknown procedure" rather than as  nothing at all.They were compiled the ordinary way for the era, with cc -qixm.Provenance: everything came from the Microware OS-9 hobbyist archive, theHamburg OS-9 Users Group, and other public preservation sites. The originalarchives are kept in CMDS/archives/ -- ed, elvis and grep include full Csource in there. Per-package documentation is in DOC/ (see DOC/README).SOURCES.txt carries fuller per-command provenance and licences.One command here is a build made for this disk rather than an archivebinary: ls, a repaired build of GNU fileutils 3.13, which builds fromSRC/ls.  wc's provenance is unsettled -- see its entry below; no sourcefor it has been found.cat, basename and dirname are archive binaries from Martin Gregorie'ssh75 package, along with sh itself; all three use cio.------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS -- the command set------------------------------------------------------ *break          send a BREAK on a serial line -- an assembler example, and                 it calls F$SysDbg, the system-debugger trap, on its way                 there.  On a machine with a debugger attached that drops you                 into it and waits for an answer, which in a script is a hang  btop          convert characters to bit patterns -- its own Function: line,                and what it does: `btop <file>' prints each character as a                grid of O and space. *epson         spline output driver for an Epson printer  ptob          convert bit patterns back to characters -- the other half of                `btop', and the round trip is exact.  ptxm           Path Table eXtension Module: a kernel extension letting                 user-state processes open unlimited I/O paths. Courtesyware,                 free. It installs into the kernel and so needs supervisor                 state. DOC/ptxm/ptxm.txt  ptxminst       install Ptxm -- the kernel extension above *read_mail      a small mail reader of its own: it opens                 /dd/MAIL/mail_<user> and offers `[L]ist again, e[X]it &                 delete mail, exit & [N]ot delete'  umusek        UMusEK -- a music editor.  It needs a hardware graphics                screen: point it at one and it opens.  Without a graphics                screen it stops with `***DS_ScAdd Error 208.' and                `Fran: Can't get screen addr, 'bye!'.  vecho          System V `echo': the newline is suppressed by a trailing \c                 in the argument, not by default. `vecho one' writes `one'                 and a CR; `vecho one\c' writes `one' and stops. Several 