0=<this disk>  os9exec ...OS-9 filenames are case-insensitive, so /h0/games/hack/playgroundfinds GAMES/HACK/playground.Seven programs use SNOBOL4-in-C, a C library thatsimulates SNOBOL4's pattern matcher, with programs translated out ofGimpel's "Algorithms in SNOBOL4".  Each is an ordinary 68k program.The library and its makefile are in SRC/effo_snobol, the package's ownnotes in DOC/snobol.  blackjak, poker, rpoem, rstory, rstory2, stoneand tformat are the seven.larn resolves the same way: it wants /h0/GAMES/larn/PLAYGROUND, andGAMES/LARN/PLAYGROUND is on the disk.  It holds the scoreboard larnwrites there for itself, plus .larn.help, .lfortune and .larnmaze.larn plays: it lays out a level, shows its '?' help, keeps score andtakes commands.Those three data files came from the larn 12.2p4 sources; the binaryreports 12.0.  SOURCES.txt records where they came from.  Its siblingularn is complete and also runs.  gnuchessc and gnuan read their datafrom GNUCHESS4.0/MISC and both start.DOC/DEPENDS lists, per program, every absolute path its binarycarries -- read it before copying single commands elsewhere.See DOC/REBUILT-NOTES.md for four curated binaries that wereoverwritten during installation and what was done about each.------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS -- added utilities (345)------------------------------------------------------  almanac        computes where the Sun, Moon and the eight planets are for a                 given date and time: right ascension, declination, apparent                 diameter and distance in AU.  Add your longitude and latitude                 and it also gives azimuth and altitude.  Its constants were                 taken from The Astronomical Almanac 1990, so accuracy softens                 the further you go from then  bc             an arbitrary-precision calculator: the numbers are as long as                 they need to be, and `scale' says how many decimal places 