21 module                             not found'.So everything up to the assembler is here and works; the assembler andlinker are Microware's.  GCC139/gcc forks a module named `os9exec' andfails earlier -- use gcc2.  GCC139 (6) gcc gcc_cc1 gcc_cc1plus gcc_cccp gcc_collect gpp             Use CMDS/makeinfo (GCC139/makeinfo was the same file).  GCC2   (8) cc1plus cc2 cc2plus cccp2 collect gcc gcc2 gpp------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/REBUILT -- alternates kept beside the originals------------------------------------------------------  Alternates for names this disk already uses, other editions of the  same program, and builds for other processors.  The curated binary  is still the one in /dd/CMDS; these are here so you can compare or  swap them yourself.  The gzip set is for other CPUs.  CMDS/gzip is 1.2.2 for the 68000.  These are 1.2.4 built six ways -- 68000, 68020 and CPU32, each with  and without csl.  If your machine is a 68020 or a CPU32, one of  these is built for it and CMDS/gzip is not.  compress_4.0   compress 4.0, another edition of CMDS/compress  zoo_2.1        zoo 2.1 (1991), newer than the 2.01 in CMDS; it reads what                 2.01 writes and has an extended help under `zoo_2.1 H' *gzip68k_csl    gzip 1.2.4, 68000, needs csl *gzip020_csl    gzip 1.2.4, 68020, needs csl *gzipcpu32k_csl  gzip 1.2.4, CPU32, needs csl  ctags.elvis    elvis 1.7's ctags; CMDS/ctags is the BSD one  elvis_input    elvis under its `input' personality -- it opens already in                 insert mode. The name is load-bearing: elvis's wrapper picks                 its personality from the last letter of the name it was                 invoked by, so a name ending in another letter falls through                 to plain vi. CMDS/input is a different program entirely  diff_1.1       a second build of GNU diff 1.1 with the same options; see                 `diff'  gtar           another build of GNU tar, taki